# Prefer lldb --batch for Debug Scripts

When writing debug scripts or automating crash/bug investigation, always use `lldb --batch` rather than interactive sessions or ad-hoc shell commands.

## Command pattern

```bash
lldb --batch \
  -o "target create ./MyBinary" \
  -o "run [args]" \
  -o "bt" \
  -o "quit" \
  -- ./MyBinary [args]
```

Or via a script file:

```bash
lldb --batch -s debug_session.lldb -- ./MyBinary
```

Where `debug_session.lldb` contains one command per line:

```
target create ./MyBinary
breakpoint set --name CrashingFunction
run
bt all
quit
```

## Rules

- Use `--batch` for any scripted, CI, or reproducible debug session — never rely on an interactive REPL in automation.
- Use `-s <script>` for multi-step sessions; use `-o` for short one-liners.
- Always end batch scripts with `quit` to ensure a clean exit code.
- Capture output by redirecting stdout: `lldb --batch ... > debug_output.txt 2>&1`.
- For crash reproduction, pair with `--core <corefile>` when a core dump is available.

## Examples

```bash
# ❌ BAD — interactive, not reproducible
lldb ./game_binary

# ✅ GOOD — batch, scriptable, CI-friendly
lldb --batch -o "target create ./game_binary" -o "run" -o "bt" -o "quit"

# ✅ GOOD — core dump analysis
lldb --batch --core crash.core -o "bt all" -o "quit" ./game_binary > crash_report.txt 2>&1
```
